Hmm, camera.rotateX after lookAt rotates about the camera's local X. Since lookAt sets no roll, that's world-X-ish. Then we add bc.roll afterwards; the code currently does `camera.rotation.z += bc.roll`. If I rotateX in between, fine.

But after pitch rotation, changing fov doesn't change orientation, so reproject and only adjust pitch again if fov changed. The loop handles it.

Dolly back: `const back = camera.getWorldDirection(v).multiplyScalar(-1)` → move pos along -dir (horizontal component? include y? If we move along -dir exactly, the look direction stays the same → pitch unchanged, vertical angles for anchors change slightly). Move both camera.position and bc.pos? If we only move the camera but not bc.pos, next frame the code sets camera.position = bc.pos again → the dolly is lost each frame, causing a 1-frame flicker? Actually the framing runs each frame after `camera.position.copy(bc.pos)`, so it recomputes the dolly each frame deterministically. Stable. But `updateWind` uses `camera.position.z` before the camera update (it runs earlier in animate) — one frame lag, negligible.

