Hmm, the guard also recently *only* checks rings with `state.active`; all 8. Let me debug the guard's behavior: it seems it dollied back and widened, but still couldn't fit the left side because the camera x=5 and yaw point left; dollying back along the view direction increases x (5→7.08) proportionally, so the *relative* geometry stays the same! Dollying along the view ray doesn't change the angular size of the field around the ray axis at all (that's the point). So if the field is clipped due to being off-axis (yaw pointing away from the field center), dollying back doesn't help.

Right: the field isn't centered on the view ray (the camera aims at a look x=0 while positioned x=5); with a narrow portrait hfov, the off-axis field can't fit.

So the horizontal overflow in portrait is due to the yaw offset, not just distance. To fix, the guard must also damp the authored *lateral* camera offset/yaw when it's too extreme for portrait. I.e. for portrait, pull the camera x toward 0 and aim at the field center.

Hmm. Options:
- In portrait, clamp the camera x to something small (e.g. ±1.5) and set the look x to ~0. This preserves the z/y framing but removes the lateral cinematic moves. On a narrow screen, lateral moves are less valuable anyway.
- Or dolly back much more (distance reduces angular offsets), e.g. z→30. But the off-axis angle is set by atan(dx/dz); increasing distance reduces it. Dollying along the *world -z* (not view ray) would reduce the offset. At z 22.9, x 7.08, the field center is at x=0 → the angle to the field center is atan(7.08/22.9)=17.2° > hfov/2 (15°) → clipped. Need z ≥ 25.4, or reduce x.

