The heartbeat has filters: `.hb-track` filter drop-shadow + `.hb-glow` blur + `.hb-line` drop-shadow; plus mix-blend-mode screen, multiple animations. These can be costly full-screen. Optimizations preserving quality:
- Move `filter: drop-shadow` off `.hb-track` (redundant with line glow) and remove `.hb-glow` blur filter by using a pre-blurred stroke with low opacity (already glow layer with 17px width). Remove `filter: blur(11px)` from `.hb-glow`; set `stroke-width:22` and opacity lower; visually similar.
- Use `will-change: transform, opacity` only on tracked elements not a filter.
- Add `contain: strict` to `#heartbeat`? Since fixed full screen, `contain: layout paint` could help.
- On mobile (`max-width:900px`), hide `.hb-ghost` and `.hb-split` (decorative layers) and skip `hb-lurch`? Keep main. This reduces 2 path animations.
- `prefers-reduced-motion`: no animations.
- Use `transform: translateZ(0)` to promote.

In JS, the heartbeat beat class removal/adding triggers 9 animations at once. That's intended quality. Could batch by toggling an attribute and use CSS animations; same.

We can also drive the heartbeat with Web Animations? not needed.

Implement CSS overrides at end:
```css
#heartbeat{contain:layout paint; isolation:isolate; transform:translateZ(0)}
.hb-glow{filter:none;stroke-width:23;opacity:.55}
.hb-line{filter:drop-shadow(...)} keep one.
.hb-track{filter:none;will-change:transform}
@media (max-width:900px){ .hb-ghost,.hb-split{display:none} .hb-track{height:...} #heartbeat.beat .hb-rings i:nth-child(3){animation:none} ...}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce){ all hb animations none }
```
But `.hb-glow` opacity is 0.30 via stroke rgba; at 23px no blur might look like a thick line; okay, "glow" less soft but still. Could use `stroke: rgba(...,.18)` and 26px to simulate.

Also the `.hb-wash` background uses two radial gradients changing position each beat via `--hb-y`; CSS transition background doesn't animate gradients; it jumps (fine) though full-screen repaint. The overlay `#heartbeat` with mix-blend-mode screen forces compositing of entire screen; could be heavy. We can add `will-change:opacity` and isolate. Maybe leave.

